policies
--------

An IAM policy is a document that defines what actions are allowed or denied on which AWS resources.
                                        or
policies are nothing but a permissions here we can define what actions a role can perform or who can access in the form of JSON.

        In simple words, a policy tells AWS:
            WHO needs access (the identity or principal involved)
            WHAT actions they can perform
            WHICH resources they can access
            Whether the action is Allowed or Denied
    Example:
        permission ppolicy
        ------------------
            "Version": "2012:10:17
            "Statement":
            {
                Effect: "allow"
                "Action": "s3:GetObject",
                "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-company-bucket/*"
            }
        Trust Polocy
        ------------
            "Version": "2012:10:17"
            "Statement": 
            {
                "Effect": "allow"
                "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
                "Principal":
                [
                    arn
                ]
            }

        Note:  you cannot use the trust policy to grant S3 access by adding the S3 bucket ARN to Resource. Trust policies and permission policies have different purposes.

